Title: "Spec Literals And `satisfies`"
Id: "27e83c61-83ff-4f00-8bc0-15775881b72d"

Text: "A spec literal is a value of builtin type `\\specification` with anonymous
subject `_`.

```mlg-fragment
_ is \real
_ \"in\" R
_ \"in\" \reals
```

The old `? is Type` and `? \"op\" Target` spellings are rejected. The anonymous
placeholder is always `_`; other uses of `?`, including inferred command
arguments and predicate operators, are unchanged.

The infix `satisfies` operator applies a specification to a subject:

```mlg-fragment
x satisfies (_ is \real)
```

This means `x is \real`. The right side may be a concrete spec literal or any
expression of type `\\specification`.

Structural type literals use a complete `_` specification at every leaf:

```mlg-fragment
(_ is \natural, _ \"in\" \reals)
{_ is \natural : ...}
(_ is \natural) -> (_ is \real)
(_ is \natural, _ \"in\" \reals) -> (_ is \real)
```

Function types use `->`, require parenthesized input and output specifications,
accept one or more input specs, and have exactly one output spec. Function
values use the distinct `=>` arrow."
Id: "a61f4faf-5784-4f99-9044-c43f46b750da"
